      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2012 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Protein tyrosine phosphatase 1B (PTP1B) is a major negative regulator of both insulin and leptin signals. For years, inhibiting of PTP1B has been considered to be a potential therapeutics for treating Type 2 diabetes and obesity. Recently, we recognized lithocholic acid (LCA) as a natural inhibitor against PTP1B (IC(50)=12.74 μM) by a vertical screen for the first time. Further SAR research was carried out by synthesizing and evaluating a series of compounds bearing two methyls at C-4 position and a fused heterocycle to ring A. Among them, compound 14b achieved a PTP1B inhibitory activity about eightfold than LCA and a 14-fold selectivity over the homogenous enzyme TCPTP."
      },
